{"t":"msg","chat":"m2resume","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Establish a regular sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, including weekends, to align your body's internal clock.\n\n2. Develop a relaxing pre-sleep routine that allows you to unwind, such as reading a book or taking a warm bath, which cues your brain to prepare for rest.\n\n3. Optimize your sleep environment by keeping your bedroom cool, dark, and quiet, and using a comfortable mattress and pillows tailored to how you sleep.\n\n4. Minimize exposure to blue light from electronic devices for at least an hour before bed, as it suppresses melatonin and delays sleepiness.\n\n5. Avoid consuming caffeine and other stimulants in the late afternoon and evening so they do not linger in your system and disrupt your sleep.\n\n6. Incorporate regular physical activity into your daily routine, but avoid intense exercise close to bedtime to allow your body time to wind down.\n\n7. Be mindful of your evening diet by avoiding heavy, spicy, or large meals right before bed, and reduce fluid intake to prevent nighttime bathroom trips.\n\n8. If you cannot fall asleep within twenty minutes, get out of bed and do a quiet, low-stress activity until you feel drowsy, then return to sleep.\n\n9. Reduce mental stimulation before bed through relaxation techniques like deep breathing, meditation, or journaling to calm an active mind.\n\n10. Reserve your bed solely for sleep and intimacy, avoiding work or other active hobbies in bed to strengthen the mental link between bed and rest."}],"seq":817}
{"t":"end","chat":"m2resume","seq":816,"stream_type":"message"}
{"t":"msg","chat":"m2resume","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Establish a regular sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, including weekends, to align your body's internal clock.\n\n2. Develop a relaxing pre-sleep routine that allows you to unwind, such as reading a book or taking a warm bath, which cues your brain to prepare for rest.\n\n3. Optimize your sleep environment by keeping your bedroom cool, dark, and quiet, and using a comfortable mattress and pillows tailored to how you sleep.\n\n4. Minimize exposure to blue light from electronic devices for at least an hour before bed, as it suppresses melatonin and delays sleepiness.\n\n5. Avoid consuming caffeine and other stimulants in the late afternoon and evening so they do not linger in your system and disrupt your sleep.\n\n6. Incorporate regular physical activity into your daily routine, but avoid intense exercise close to bedtime to allow your body time to wind down.\n\n7. Be mindful of your evening diet by avoiding heavy, spicy, or large meals right before bed, and reduce fluid intake to prevent nighttime bathroom trips.\n\n8. If you cannot fall asleep within twenty minutes, get out of bed and do a quiet, low-stress activity until you feel drowsy, then return to sleep.\n\n9. Reduce mental stimulation before bed through relaxation techniques like deep breathing, meditation, or journaling to calm an active mind.\n\n10. Reserve your bed solely for sleep and intimacy, avoiding work or other active hobbies in bed to strengthen the mental link between bed and rest."}],"seq":817}
